"Algebraic properties of Levi graphs associated with curve arrangements (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40687-022-00325-3)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; One of the most important information that each d-arrangement carries is the intersection poset which decodes the incidences between curves and intersection points. ... Our expectation is that this generalization of Terao’s conjecture should be false for \\(d\\geqslant 2\\), mostly due to the fact that there are examples of conic-line arrangements in the plane with ordinary singularities providing counterexamples to a generalized Terao’s conjecture (see [21] for details).\nSince all the intersection points of d-arrangements are ordinary, they behave analytically like line arrangements around the intersection points. This observation motivates us to introduce the notion of the Levi graph for d-arrangements.\n\n### Definition 2.2\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal {C}\\) be a d-arrangement. Then the Levi graph \\(G = (V,E)\\) is a bipartite graph with \\(V : = V_{1} \\cup V_{2} = \\{x_{1}, ..., x_{s}, y_{1}, ..., y_{k}\\}\\), where each vertex \\(y_{i}\\) corresponds to a curve \\(C_{i}\\), each vertex \\(x_{j}\\) corresponds to an intersection point \\(p_{j} \\in \\textrm{Sing}(\\mathcal {C})\\) and \\(x_{j}\\) is joined with \\(y_{i}\\) by an edge in E if and only if \\(p_{j}\\) is incident with \\(C_{i}\\).\n\nThe importance of the Levi graphs for d-arrangements can be observed in the context of a naive generalization of Terao’s conjecture on the freeness of d-arrangements. For the completeness of the exposition, let us present this conjecture.\n\n### Conjecture 2.3\n\n(Generalized Terao conjecture) Let \\(\\mathcal {C}_{1},\\mathcal {C}_{2} \\subset \\mathbb {P}^{2}_{\\mathbb {C}}\\) be two d-arrangements for a fixed \\(d\\geqslant 1\\) and denote by \\(G_{1}, G_{2}\\) the associated Levi graphs. Assume that \\(G_{1}\\) and \\(G_{2}\\) are isomorphic, is it true that the freenees of \\(\\mathcal {C}_{1}\\) implies that \\(\\mathcal {C}_{2}\\) is also free?\n\nOur expectation is that this generalization of Terao’s conjecture should be false for \\(d\\geqslant 2\\), mostly due to the fact that there are examples of conic-line arrangements in the plane with ordinary singularities providing counterexamples to a generalized Terao’s conjecture (see [21] for details).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA341822 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A341822)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.7 years ago; Crawled: 3.5 years ago; Length of the longest 2-increasing sequence of positive integer triples with entries <= n. ... Terms n <= 5 have been confirmed by brute-force search (Table 1 of Gowers and Long (2021)).\n\n(Greetings from The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences!)\n\nA341822\n\nLength of the longest 2-increasing sequence of positive integer triples with entries <= n.\n\n0\n\n1, 2, 4, 8, 10, 14, 17, 21, 27, 30, 35\n\nOFFSET\n\n1,2\n\nCOMMENTS\n\nA triple t=(a_1,a_2,a_3) is defined to be 2-less than a triple u=(b_1,b_2,b_3) if a_i < b_i for at least two coordinates i. A sequence t^(j) of triples is 2-increasing if for all i < j, t^(i) is 2-less than t^(j).\n\nTerms n <= 5 have been confirmed by brute-force search (Table 1 of Gowers and Long (2021)).\n\nREFERENCES\n\nW. T. Gowers and J. Long, The length of an s-increasing sequence of r-tuples, Combinatorics, Probability and Computing 30 (2021), 1-36.\n\nLINKS\n\nTable of n, a(n) for n=1..11.\n\nW. T. Gowers and J. Long, The length of an s-increasing sequence of r-tuples, arXiv:1609.08688 [math.CO], 2016.\n\nPo-Shen Loh, Directed paths: from Ramsey to Ruzsa and Szemerédi, arXiv:1505.07312 [math.CO], 2015.\n\nFORMULA\n\na(n) >= n^{3/2} when n is a perfect square.\n\nIt is conjectured that a(n) <= n^{3/2} for all n.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nFor n=4, the sequence (1,1,1), (1,2,2), (2,1,3), (2,2,4), (3,3,1), (3,4,2), (4,3,3), (4,4,4) has length a(4)=8 and every 2-increasing sequence of length 9 must contain a triple with some coordinate equal to 5.\n\nCROSSREFS\n\nCf. A000093.\n\nSequence in context: A153974 A290476 A189670 * A363797 A034822 A300781\n\nAdjacent sequences: A341819 A341820 A341821 * A341823 A341824 A341825\n\nKEYWORD\n\nnonn,hard,more\n\nAUTHOR\n\nMarcel K. Goh, Feb 20 2021\n\nEXTENSIONS\n\nEdited by N. J. A. Sloane, Mar 21 2021\n\na(10)-a(11) and confirmation of previous terms by Bert Dobbelaere, Mar 27 2021\n\nSTATUS\n\napproved--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe length of an s-increasing sequence of r-tuples (https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.08688)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.1 years ago; Gowers, J. ... Loh asks how long a 2-increasing sequence can be if all the triples take values in $\\{1,2,\\dots,n\\}$, and gives a $\\log_*$ improvement over the trivial upper bound of $n^2$ by using the triangle removal lemma.\nTitle: The length of an s-increasing sequence of r-tuples\nAuthors: W. T. Gowers, J. Long\nDate: Tue Sep 27 22:17:02 2016\n\nWe prove a number of results related to a problem of Po-Shen Loh, which is equivalent to a problem in Ramsey theory. Let $a=(a_1,a_2,a_3)$ and $b=(b_1,b_2,b_3)$ be two triples of integers. Define $a$ to be 2-less than $b$ if $a_i<b_i$ for at least two values of $i$, and define a sequence $a^1,\\dots,a^m$ of triples to be 2-increasing if $a^r$ is 2-less than $a^s$ whenever $r<s$. Loh asks how long a 2-increasing sequence can be if all the triples take values in $\\{1,2,\\dots,n\\}$, and gives a $\\log_*$ improvement over the trivial upper bound of $n^2$ by using the triangle removal lemma. In the other direction, a simple construction gives a lower bound of $n^{3/2}$. We look at this problem and a collection of generalizations, improving some of the known bounds, pointing out connections to other well known problems in extremal combinatorics, and asking a number of further questions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMonochromatic Monotone Path Problems and (3, 2)-Sequences (https://math-projects.elte.hu/media/works/445/report/ELTE_Individual_Project_2025_1st_semester.pdf)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Conjecture 3.2 (Uniform Coordinate Distribution). ... 2-increasing property is kept. ... Also, we note that Gowers and Long wrote about similar swapping ideas in their paper [2].\n3\nProject Contributions and Conjectures\n3.1\nComputational Verification and Extension\nWe wrote a program to verify known exact values of F(n) for n ∈[5] and extended our search up to\nn = 9. Had we found any example where F(n) > n3/2 that would have immediately given us a better\nbound for larger n values too as explained by a lemma in [2]. For F(9) = 27 we generated all the\noptimal constructions (there are 874776 of them). These have particularly nice structures and they\nhelped us to come up with conjectures and verify them programatically.\n3.2\nStructural Conjectures\nConjecture 3.1 (Tightness of the Asymptotic Bound, also conjectured in the other papers). The\nlower bound is tight: F(n) = O(n3/2).\nConjecture 3.2 (Uniform Coordinate Distribution). For any perfect square n, any optimal sequence\nof length F(n) has the property that each value k ∈[n] appears exactly √n times in each coordinate.\nDefinition 3.1 (Cut). A sequence of triples x1, x2, . . . , xL is said to have a cut in the kth coordinate\nif there exists an index i ∈{1, . . . , L −1} such that for every pair of indices j ≤i and m > i, the k-th\ncoordinate is strictly increasing, i.e., xj,k < xm,k.\nConjecture 3.3 (Decomposition via Cuts). Every optimal (3, 2)-Sequence has a Cut in at least one\nof the coordinates.\n3.3\nConditional Upper Bound\nLemma 3.1. Let F ′(n) be the maximal length of a (3, 2)-Sequence with the additional constraint that\none coordinate must be monotone increasing. Then F ′(n) ≤n3/2. (See proof sketch in Appendix)\nThis lemma motivates the following proof technique. We start from an optimal sequence. Then\nwe start swapping pairs of coordinates between elements (details are omitted here) in a way that the\n2-increasing property is kept. After some swaps we achieve our goal to make one of the coordinates\nmonotone increasing. At that point we can just use our lemma to finish the upper bound proof.\nWe programatically checked for random optimal sequences that this swapping procedure is always\npossible. Also, we note that Gowers and Long wrote about similar swapping ideas in their paper [2].\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExact values F(6)=14 and F(7)=17 for a function of Gowers and Long (https://airr.science/papers/ARR-2026-2HHHW35HE98QVA5B/versions/v1/)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Short computational note claiming F(6)=14 and F(7)=17 for the Gowers-Long 2-increasing triple problem. ...     ★★☆☆☆The paper says it is a small computational datum that neither proves nor refutes Conjecture 1.8, and covers only n<=7.\n\nanthropic · claude-haiku-5-52026-10-22 05:02:15 UTC+02:00 · not involved in manuscript · review context: unknown★★☆☆☆☆☆☆☆☆2.00\n\nRecommendation: Major Revision · Material objections: 2\n\nShort computational note claiming F(6)=14 and F(7)=17 for the Gowers-Long 2-increasing triple problem. The lower-bound witnesses are correct (I re-checked both). The F(6)=14 upper bound has a reasonable evidence base: branch-and-bound, two SAT solvers, and a DRUP proof accepted by a second checker. The F(7)=17 upper bound is only partly certified: 18 of 425 proofs are unchecked, there is no complete certificate, and an earlier encoding bug was found. Theorem 1 does not reflect this. Recommendation: major revision.\n\nCorrectness confidence\n    ★★☆☆☆Both lower-bound witnesses (n=6 length 14, n=7 length 17) re-checked by me: valid 2-increasing sequences in range. F(1)-F(4) reproduced by my own search. Upper bound F(6)<=14 has several independent checks. Upper bound F(7)<=17 rests on 407 of 425 checked DRUP proofs; 18 largest unchecked, plus an earlier cube-encoding bug.\n    ★★☆☆☆Scripts (cube.py, proofcubes.py, bnb.go, rupcheck), the Lean file and a 2.7 GB proof supplement are cited but are not in my folder, so I could not inspect or rerun them. The witnesses are printed and I re-checked them. The n=7 upper-bound pipeline cannot be reproduced from the paper text alone.\n\n#### Strengths\n\n  * Lower-bound witnesses for n=6 (14) and n=7 (17) are valid 2-increasing sequences in range; I re-checked both.\n  * A status table separates rigorous, computational and partly proof-checked claims.\n  * F(6)<=14 is supported by an independent branch-and-bound program, CP-SAT and CaDiCaL, and a DRUP proof accepted by a second checker that also rejects five false proofs.\n  * Symmetry reduction and partition arithmetic are internally consistent: 84 sorted first elements, 83 excluding (1,1,1), 77 second elements, 312 subcubes of (1,1,2); 471 = 425+46 and 407+18 = 425.\n  * The paper discloses AI-assisted production, the absence of human review and the limits of its verification.\n\n#### Weaknesses\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTriangular arrangements on the projective plane (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/379849498_Triangular_arrangements_on_the_projective_plane)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; because, given a triangular arrangement, the proof of Theorem 3.2 implies in ﬁ nitely many associated RUAs.\n\nObserve that this would give further evidence towards Terao’s conjecture, as it is a special case. ... In this paper we focus on various aspects of singular complex plane curves, mostly in the context of their homological properties and the associated combinatorial structures. ... However, except the case of line arrangements, we are not aware of